  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nTonight through Monday...\n\nA few spotty showers and sprinkles lingered over the forecast area \nthis evening, with temperatures finally feeling crisp and fall-like, \nin the upper 50s. \n\nQuiet nighttime skies will be interrupted by a line of convection \ndeveloping along an elevated boundary early Monday morning, from \nsoutheast through northeast Nebraska. While a strong storm or two \ncan't be totally ruled out, weak instability and the elevated nature \nof the storms will likely limit large hail and strong wind \nproduction. \n\nA stout low level jet will help advect warmer air into the region \ntomorrow afternoon, as morning convection shifts off to the east. \nExpect scattered showers with a few embedded rumbles of thunder to \ndevelop across the forecast area tomorrow afternoon, as a moist \nunstable airmass builds into the Central Plains. Highs tomorrow \nafternoon will top out in the 80s, with a few locations reaching the \nlow 90s in far southeast Nebraska. However, this warm-up will be \nbrief, as a cold front approaches from the west Monday afternoon and \nevening. Out next conditional threat for severe weather will develop \nahead of this front, in a fairly impressive environment. Forecast \nsoundings indicate 2000-3000+ J/kg of CAPE, steep lapse rates, 30-\n40kts of low and deep layer shear, as well as notable low level \nturning in hodographs. These would suggest that all severe modes, \nincluding tornadoes, would be on the table. That being said, it \ndoesn't look like a slam dunk widespread severe event. Daytime cloud \ncover could lower temperatures a bit and strengthen the cap. \nAdditionally, if multiple storms do break through the cap and \ndevelop along the front at once, they could quickly grow upscale and \ncongeal into more of a blob, as opposed to strong discrete cells. \nAdditionally, strong downbursts could collapse updrafts before the \nstorms can establish a strong mesocyclone, capable of producing \nlarge hail, damaging winds, or a tornado. Overall, it appears the \nmain threat for severe potential would be during the first 1-3 hours \nafter convective initiation, when more discrete storms, with robust \nrotating updrafts would be possible. \n\nInitial potentially destructive storms will quickly push east of the \nforecast area Monday evening, with more widespread, but less severe \nconvection filling in behind them. Heavy rainfall will be something \nto keep an eye on throughout the evening and overnight hours. While \ndeep warm cloud layers and high PWATs indicate storms will be \nefficient rain producers, the overall progressive movement of the \nstorms should help lower flooding risks. That being said, there \nremains a 25% risk of flash flooding within a Slight Excessive \nRainfall Outlook area, highlighting areas along and east of the \nMissouri River.\n\nTuesday and Beyond...\n\nShower and storm chances will continue behind the front overnight \nand into Tuesday, as the LLJ noses back into the region. The best \nchance remaining across southeast Nebraska into southwest Iowa. \nTemperatures will be noticeably cooler Tuesday, as we slip back into \nthe 70s.\n\nSimilarly wet and cool conditions will remain in place as the \nboundary lingers in the region through at least the middle of next \nweek, enhanced by a few shortwave disturbances rippling through the \nsouth westerly flow. Abundant moisture training over the same \nlocations this week could potentially raise flooding concerns once \nagain, however, the gradual, drawn out nature of the rainfall should \nhelp. \n\nTemps will remain in the 70s through at least Thursday, with \nslightly warmer conditions expected Friday, as a subtle ridge \ndevelops ahead of the next trough and cold front. Long range models \ncontinue to hint at daily rain chances through next weekend, with \ntemperatures dipping back below seasonal levels.",
